{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792694983,"nonce":"uaE7nYQ0rUr1lp2LUkriqetH01I=","message":"Drop-glue approach, from a read-only survey of Rayzor's instance freeing (unverified by running anything).\n\nRayzor frees instances with a conservative whole-module compile-time pass (InsertFreePass: escape and last-use analysis, transfer of fresh returned objects) plus a runtime helper that frees owned children recursively. Its key difference from Zyntax's drop_glue: a field is owned only if every store to it anywhere in the module is a fresh single-holder allocation (a per-field store census); any unproven store leaves the field borrowed, so aliasing leaks instead of double-freeing. It also checks that loads out of the owner stay confined (a value read from the owner may alias the child) and guards against releasing twice. Nullable fields are null pointers, skipped at free. Arrays of instances free only the buffer, so elements leak. There is no refcount and no cycle handling: cycles are argued impossible because a cycle needs an alias, which is never owned. Its pass has no switch (it runs at every level) and no tests in the tree.\n\nZyntax drop_glue treats every named reference field as owned and runs only with ZYNTAX_DROP_GLUE=1; it skips Optional\u003cNamed\u003e tagged unions and has no per-field proof.\n\nRecommendation: keep Zyntax's per-type synthesised glue (no 64-field cap, no runtime lookup) and add the ownership proof (fresh_single_holder, compute_owned_fields, compute_returns_fresh_objects equivalents) in the compiler, so glue is safe to run by default for every frontend. Niche pointer for T | null. For Array\u003cT\u003e, add an element loop only when the array is proven owned and its elements fresh. Do not copy the runtime mask. Code with shared nodes or parent pointers leaks, as in Rayzor; only a Zyntax-specific refcount would fix that.\n\nSequencing: slice 1 (scalar-field classes) needs no glue. Slice 1b depends on the proof in the compiler.","files":null}]}